The time period of a simple pendulum is given by $T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}$, where $L$ is the length of the pendulum and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity. The length of the pendulum will change with temperature. The coefficient of linear expansion is $\alpha = 16 \times 10^{-6}$ C$^{-1}$. The temperature difference is $\Delta T = 30^\circ C - 20^\circ C = 10^\circ C$.

The change in length is $\Delta L = \alpha L \Delta T = (16 \times 10^{-6})(L)(10) = 16 \times 10^{-5} L$.
The new length is $L' = L + \Delta L = L + 16 \times 10^{-5} L = L(1 + 16 \times 10^{-5})$.
The new time period is $T' = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L'}{g}} = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L(1 + 16 \times 10^{-5})}{g}} = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} \sqrt{1 + 16 \times 10^{-5}} = T \sqrt{1 + 16 \times 10^{-5}}$.

The time lost per period is $\Delta t = T - T' = T - T \sqrt{1 + 16 \times 10^{-5}} = T(1 - \sqrt{1 + 16 \times 10^{-5}})$.
